<h1>Astra's microGPT in Fortress: independent review, then comparison with ours</h1>
<p class="dek">Two phases, strictly ordered. Phase 1 was written to <code>phase1.md</code> before anything of ours was opened. Every claim below is backed by a run in this worktree (<code>runs/*.log</code>), a probe (<code>explorations/astra-run/Rvw*.fss</code>), or a line-cited spec/library source. Nothing was committed; the main checkout was not touched.</p>

<div class="verdict">
<p><b>One-paragraph verdict.</b> Astra's program runs, its 228-parameter fixture validates here on a fresh run, its goldens are bit-identical to the real upstream Python executed under the same conventions (my harness — a check Astra's own record lacks), and every spec citation it makes is accurate at the cited line. Its rendered notation is honest and verbatim-sliced, but it never found two things the specification provides — the postfix <code>^T</code> and the second juxtaposition direction — so its attention line is a Σ inside a constructor where ours is the paper's line. Ours wins most rendered pairs (attention, loss, embedding, concat, forward, max) and loses two: Astra's tight-fraction spelling gives rmsnorm and softmax the paper's stacked layout, and Astra typesets Adam while ours never renders it. On correctness anchoring Astra wins outright (228 gradients + Adam step + sampling vs our 5 gradients). One factual correction lands on our side: our ledger calls chained subscripting a design limit; the spec grammar says it is an interpreter bug, as Astra classified it.</p>
</div>

<h4>Environment</h4>
<p class="small">Worktree <code>/home/user/fortress/.claude/worktrees/agent-a6ad2bea3bd03e8b7</code>, prebuilt classes and <code>default_repository</code> copied from the main checkout, JDK 25, <code>FORTRESS_THREADS=1</code>, <code>JAVA_FLAGS='-Xmx512m -Xss32m'</code> (Astra's flags; the launcher's default is <code>-Xmx256m -Xss32m</code>). Astra's tree from <code>git archive origin/codex/astra-microgpt experiment</code>. Rasterization: headless Chromium for the two articles, <code>rsvg-convert</code> for our per-block SVGs; Astra ships PNGs.</p>

