<p>Both confirmed. The subscript is invoked with zero arguments when followed by <code>^</code> or by another <code>[…]</code>.</p>
<h4>Code-quality faults</h4>
<ul>
<li><code>K = Mat(t+1,s,fn (u,j) ⇒ (head(C.keys[u],h,s))[j])</code> allocates a fresh <code>head</code> vector for every (u,j) cell — O(s²) per cached row.</li>
<li>Verification plumbing in the model core: <code>attention</code> returns <code>(weights, output)</code> and <code>transformer</code> writes <code>C.weights[t H + h]</code> only so the driver can print attention weights; <code>transformer</code> takes <code>C, t, H</code>.</li>
<li>Fixture constants in the core: <code>0#3</code>, <code>/3.0</code>, <code>Cache(3,2)</code>, <code>gradient(loss,228)</code>; <code>model(p)</code> hard-wires the 3×4 / 4×4 / 16×4 shapes.</li>
<li>Parameters identified by a global integer index (<code>variable(i,x)</code>) rather than node identity — couples the AD to a flat array and forces <code>matrixAt</code>.</li>
<li>Recursive DFS reverse pass on a linked <code>Chain</code>; needs <code>-Xss32m</code> (acknowledged).</li>
</ul>

<h3>1.5 Pedagogy</h3>
<p>Ten numbered sections, each: prose → [Fortify render | ASCII source] → [Python comparison] → prose. No detached code dumps (full source is collapsed). MathML inline math renders in Chromium. It teaches the <i>notation construction</i> well (scalar graph → eager containers → teaching Σ to the scalar → linear algebra from Σ; the <code>except</code> story; the closing departures table). It teaches the <i>transformer</i> thinly: attention gets four sentences and no intuition, residuals / RMSnorm / ReLU one sentence each, nothing on what a language model does or why heads. The brief demanded equal weight; the article is roughly 70/30 notation-and-verification vs transformer. Tone is a compliance memo ("It is educational machinery, not an industrial autodiff engine").</p>

