<h1>The blinded Fable microGPT run: independent review, then comparison with Astra</h1>
<p class="dek">Two phases, strictly ordered. Phase 1 was written to <code>fable-review/phase1.md</code> before Astra's tree was opened; Phase 2 reuses the previous review's findings on Astra (<code>astra-review/phase1.md</code>) rather than redoing them. Every claim below cites a file in <code>explorations/blinded-fable/</code>, a run log in <code>fable-review/runs/</code>, a reviewer probe in <code>explorations/fable-review-probes/</code>, or a line in the specification or library. Nothing was committed; nothing else under the checkout was modified.</p>

<div class="verdict">
<p><b>One-paragraph verdict.</b> The blinded Fable run is the better program and the better article; Astra's is the tidier evidence trail. On the mission's forcing function — the executable definition beside the paper's formula, judged by eye — the blinded Fable run wins seven of twelve pairs and loses two, both to one typographic device (a tight <code>/</code> that Fortify sets as a stacked fraction) it never used. Its correctness anchoring is the stronger by a wide margin: the reference's actual 4,192-parameter model on real data for two training steps and three replayed samples, goldens regenerated here bit-identically from the upstream text (18,216 scalars), two alternative complete programs and a 12-step training demo all passing the same checks; Astra's is a one-step 228-parameter synthetic fixture whose derivation from upstream was only established by the previous review. Its core is smaller (162 vs 181 lines) while covering more of the Python, and it keeps fixture constants and verification plumbing out of the core where Astra does not. Where the two disagree about the language — reverse juxtaposition, concatenation as a big operator, carriers as generators — the running, verified program settles each in the blinded Fable run's favour. Its faults: an over-stated "same graph" sentence, an apples-to-oranges line-count sentence, an unused parameter, and an HTML deliverable where the brief asked for Markdown.</p>
</div>

